.TH MACHO\-FINDCALL 1 "October 2, 2026" "MachO-Tools 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
macho\-findcall \- find direct call sites and call targets in a Mach\-O
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B macho\-findcall
.I hex-vaddr
.RI [ binary | bundle ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-arch= name ]
.RB [ \-b | \-\-binary
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-\-include\-data ]
.br
.B macho\-findcall \-\-list
.RI [ binary | bundle ]
.RI [ max ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-include\-data ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Reports the direct
.B call
and
.B jmp
sites in a Mach\-O that resolve to a given virtual address, or with
.B \-\-list
the distinct addresses the binary calls directly.
.PP
Both x86_64
.B rel32
and arm64
.B BL
encodings are decoded. An architecture whose encoding is not implemented is
named in an
.B unsupported
list rather than silently reporting nothing.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-arch= name
Restrict to one architecture by name:
.BR x86_64 ,
.BR arm64 ,
.BR arm64e ,
.BR arm64_32 ,
.BR ppc ,
.BR ppc64 ,
.BR arm
or
.BR i386 .
A preference, not a requirement.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-binary " " \fIpath\fR
The binary to read.
.TP
.B \-\-include\-data
Widen the scan from sections whose attributes mark them as instructions to
every non\-empty section. This reports more sites, including some that are data
decoding as an instruction; see
.BR DIAGNOSTICS .
.TP
.B \-\-json
Write one JSON object to standard output and send the per\-slice narration to
standard error.
.TP
.I max
With
.BR \-\-list ,
the number of distinct targets to report.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MACHO_BINARY
The binary to read when none is given on the command line.
.TP
.B MACHO_APP
An application bundle to read, tried after
.BR MACHO_BINARY .
.TP
.B MACHO_CONFIG
Path to a replacement
.IR config.json .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
At least one call site was found.
.TP
.B 1
The tool ran and found nothing.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.TP
.B 3
The file could not be read, or no slice could be scanned.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Every hit is a site
.I worth
disassembling, not a proven call\-graph edge.
.PP
Only
.I direct
calls appear. An indirect call, a register call or a jump through a PLT stub
does not encode its target in the instruction, so this tool cannot see it. This
is the largest gap against a real disassembler and the reason these tools hand
work to one rather than replace it.
.PP
The arm64 scan steps four bytes at a time and therefore sees aligned
.B BL
instructions only; an instruction at an unaligned address is missed.
.PP
By default only sections marked as instructions are scanned. If a slice marks no
section as instructions at all, every non\-empty section is scanned instead and
the result reports that it fell back, because reporting zero call sites for an
unusual input is worse than an untyped scan.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
macho\-findcall 0x100085c30 /usr/local/go/bin/go
macho\-findcall \-\-list /usr/local/go/bin/go 20
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR macho\-sym (1),
.BR macho\-symlookup (1),
.BR macho\-describe (1),
.BR otool (1)